Most buyers who buy a [GIA certified marquise diamond](/marquise-cut-diamond) never actually read the certificate. They check the grade letters — G, VS2, Ideal — and stop there. They miss four specific fields that change the price by $2,000 or change the look entirely.

This guide covers those four fields. Not the full 4Cs overview. Specifically the cert fields that matter for marquise and that generic guides skip.

One note before we start: [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) does not give a Cut grade for marquise diamonds. Cut grades from GIA exist only for round brilliant. For fancy shapes, GIA reports Polish and Symmetry — but no overall Cut grade. "Ideal" and "Very Good" cut quality labels come from the retailer's own assessment. GIA does not assign them. More on this below.

## What the GIA Certificate Shows for Marquise

A GIA Diamond Grading Report for a [marquise cut diamond](/marquise-cut-diamond) includes:

- **Shape and cutting style** — Marquise, Brilliant
- **Measurements** — three dimensions in mm (Length × Width × Depth)
- **Carat weight**
- **Color grade** — D through Z scale
- **Clarity grade** — FL through I2
- **Cut grade** — NOT listed for marquise (round brilliant only)
- **Polish** — Excellent, Very Good, Good, Fair, Poor
- **Symmetry** — Excellent, Very Good, Good, Fair, Poor
- **Fluorescence** — None, Faint, Medium, Strong, Very Strong
- **Proportion diagram** — showing Table %, Depth %, Crown Angle, Pavilion Angle, Girdle
- **Clarity characteristic diagram** — map of inclusions
- **Comments** — where additional notes appear

The four fields most buyers miss: Color, Clarity, Measurements, and the Proportion section (Table % + Depth %). These separate a $4,473 stone from a $6,568 stone of the same shape.

## The 4 GIA Cert Fields That Save $2,000

### Cert Field 1: Color Grade — The G-H Sweet Spot

The [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) color scale runs D (colorless) to Z (light yellow). For round brilliants, buyers often go to I or J. The round's brilliance scatters light and masks color. Marquise works differently.

The elongated shape concentrates color at both pointed tips. The tips are the thinnest points of the stone. Color shows more strongly there than at the belly. At J color, a marquise shows more warmth at the tips. A round brilliant masks that warmth.

The practical implication: buy one grade higher in a marquise than you would in a round. If you'd buy H in a round, buy G in a marquise. If you'd buy I in a round, consider H for marquise.

**The color sweet spot for [marquise diamonds](/marquise-cut-diamond) in white metal: G-H.**

G gives a near-colorless appearance at any size. H gives a barely-perceptible warmth at 1ct that disappears in a white gold or platinum setting. Both are invisible to a casual viewer. D-F gives no visible improvement in a set stone. The setting metal and flash of light cover any difference between G and D.

**Price at 1ct, VS2 clarity, from certified market inventory:**

| Color | Price | Link | vs G savings |
|-------|-------|------|-------------|
| I | $4,049 | [View](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28386475?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | — |
| H | $4,393 | [View](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30259333?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | — |
| G | $4,607 | [View](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29985422?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Baseline |
| F | $4,473 | [View](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29764389?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Same cost |
| E | $5,603 | [View](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29213226?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | +$996 |
| D | $5,442 | [View](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28437319?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | +$835 |

**What this means for the cert:** Read the Color field. If it says G or H, you're in the sweet spot. If it says D, E, or F, ask yourself whether you're paying for a visible difference — or for a laboratory letter.

In yellow gold, the sweet spot shifts. Yellow metal warms any stone. In yellow gold, I-J color is acceptable because the metal covers warmth in the stone. In yellow gold, you're paying for colorlessness the setting hides entirely if you go G color.

### Cert Field 2: Clarity Grade — VS2 Is the Ceiling

Clarity grades on a GIA cert: FL, IF, VVS1, VVS2, VS1, VS2, SI1, SI2, I1, I2.

For marquise diamonds, the practical visibility of inclusions changes with size and face-up area. A marquise has more face-up area per carat than a round. But that larger table also exposes inclusions. The same SI1 that hides in a round is more visible in a marquise. The facet pattern scatters light differently.

The sweet spot for marquise clarity: **VS2**.

VS2 inclusions are not visible to the naked eye. They require 10× magnification to see reliably. A VS2 marquise at 1ct looks identical to a VVS2 marquise from across the table. From arm's length, VVS2 offers nothing over VS2.

**Price at 1ct, comparing clarity grades:**

| Color | Clarity | Price | What this means |
|-------|---------|-------|-----------------|
| G | VS2 | [$4,607](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29985422?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Baseline — eye-clean, ideal sweet spot |
| G | VVS2 | [$5,686](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29652787?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | +$1,079 — zero visible improvement |
| F | VS2 | [$4,473](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29764389?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Excellent value — F color at VS2 |
| F | VVS2 | [$6,568](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29719954?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | +$2,095 — this is the $2,000 gap |

The F VVS2 at $6,568 and the F VS2 at $4,473 look the same on the finger. Two different letters on the cert. Zero visible difference in a finished [marquise engagement ring](/marquise-diamond-engagement-ring).

**What to watch at SI1:** Some SI1 marquise diamonds are eye-clean. Some are not. SI1 is riskier in marquise than in round because the larger face-up area makes inclusions more visible. If you go to SI1, always view the stone under magnification first. At VS2, you don't need to worry.

**For the cert:** Read the Clarity field. VS2 is the recommended ceiling for marquise. Anything above VS2 is paying for lab documentation, not visible beauty.

### Cert Field 3: Measurements — The L/W Ratio Hidden in the Cert

The GIA cert lists three measurements in millimeters: Length × Width × Depth. For example: **9.21 × 4.63 × 2.80 mm**.

Most buyers ignore this field. It's the most important dimension data on the cert.

Divide Length by Width: 9.21 ÷ 4.63 = **1.99:1**. That's an ideal marquise L/W ratio. The stone is nearly twice as long as it is wide. That's the classic marquise proportion. It flatters fingers, maximizes face-up area, and has the strongest secondary market demand.

**Marquise L/W ratio guide from the measurements field:**

| L/W Ratio | Character | Market Demand | What this means |
|-----------|-----------|---------------|-----------------|
| 1.60–1.74:1 | Wide, stubby | Thin | Looks fat; loses the elongating visual effect |
| 1.75–1.84:1 | Shorter marquise | Moderate | Acceptable; slightly shorter than classic |
| **1.85–2.00:1** | **Classic marquise** | **Strongest** | **The ideal range for resale and finger effect** |
| 2.01–2.10:1 | Slightly elongated | Good | Still works well; most buyers happy |
| 2.11:1+ | Very narrow | Thin | Looks extreme; harder to resell |

Take measurements of 10.80 × 4.60 × 2.90 mm. That's an L/W of 10.80 ÷ 4.60 = **2.35:1**. Extremely narrow. That stone will look like a sliver on the finger. Most buyers don't want it. The cert shows you this before you buy.

A 1ct stone at 9.0–9.5 mm length and 4.5–4.8 mm width gives you the classic marquise proportion. Calculate this yourself from the cert before clicking through to price.

**The savings angle:** Marquise diamonds with extreme L/W ratios often get listed at a discount because buyer demand is lower. You can find a 1ct marquise at an extreme ratio for $400–600 less than a classically proportioned stone at the same grade. That discount is not a deal — it's a reflection of thinner demand and lower [resale value](/marquise-diamond-resale-value). The Measurements field protects you from this.

### Cert Field 4: Table % and Depth % — The Proportions That Kill Brilliance

The proportion diagram on every GIA cert includes Table % and Depth %. These are the ratios of the table facet width and total stone depth to the total stone diameter.

For marquise, the ideal ranges:
- **Table %: 53–63%** — within this range, the stone balances brilliance and fire
- **Depth %: 58–62%** — within this range, the stone returns light efficiently and shows full face-up size

A marquise cut deeper than 62% loses face-up area. The extra material hides underneath — you're paying carat weight that you can't see. A 1ct stone at 64% depth looks like a 0.88–0.92ct stone face-up.

A marquise with a table over 65% tends to look glassy. The flat table dominates. The stone loses fire (the colored flashes of light). It looks transparent rather than brilliant.

**The proportions that hurt:**
- Depth 64%+: visible size penalty; you pay for carat weight you can't see
- Table 65%+: fire reduction; stone looks flat and glassy
- Table below 50%: stone looks too dark in the center

Read the proportion diagram on the GIA cert before buying. It takes 15 seconds. A stone with Depth 60%, Table 57% is well-cut for marquise. A stone with Depth 65%, Table 67% is not — and the price doesn't always reflect that difference.

## The Color Trap

The Color Trap: paying for D-F color in a shape where D-F is invisible. No visible benefit at all.

Here is how it works. A buyer sets a budget of $6,000 for a 1ct marquise. They want the best stone. They target D or E color, the highest grades. They find a [1ct D VS2 Ideal at $5,442](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28437319?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — within budget.

They set it in a platinum solitaire. The stone looks beautiful.

What they don't know: a [1ct G VS2 Ideal at $4,607](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29985422?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) would look identical in that setting. The $835 difference pays for a letter on a piece of paper.

In the right setting, G looks like D. The platinum hides any warmth in the stone. The flash of light covers any remaining trace. A professional gemologist with a master set can tell the difference. Your friends at the table cannot.

**The Color Trap compounds when buyers choose the wrong metal.** If you're setting a marquise in 14k yellow gold, D color is genuinely wasted. The warm gold warms the stone regardless of its inherent color. H-I in yellow gold is indistinguishable from D-F in yellow gold after setting. The metal closes that gap entirely.

**Where color does matter in marquise:** In an east-west [marquise engagement ring](/marquise-diamond-engagement-ring) setting, the stone's belly is visible from the side. Color at the belly is slightly more visible than in a north-south (point-up) setting. In an east-west orientation, consider G over H at 1ct+ if using white metal. This is the one setting where color matters slightly more.

## The Depth Penalty

The Depth Penalty is what buyers pay when they choose a marquise cut at 63%+ depth. They pay for carat weight they cannot see. They lose the face-up presence that makes marquise desirable.

Here is the calculation. A [marquise cut diamond](/marquise-cut-diamond) is priced per carat. A 1ct marquise cut at 60% depth has all of its carat weight contributing to face-up area. A 1ct marquise cut at 65% depth has 5–8% of its weight hidden below the girdle. The face-up area is smaller.

The buyer pays for 1ct. They see 0.92ct's worth of face-up stone.

This is invisible in the listing unless you read the cert. The depth percentage appears in the proportion diagram. A depth of 60% is excellent. A depth of 65% is poor for marquise.

**Depth and Table % at key price levels:**

| Stone | Ideal Proportions? | Price | Notes |
|-------|-------------------|-------|-------|
| 0.75ct H VS1 Ideal | Read cert | [$1,962](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30345718?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Verify depth % before buying |
| 0.75ct G VS1 Very Good | Read cert | [$1,888](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29844405?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Check table — Very Good may still hit 58–62% |
| 1ct H VS2 Ideal | Read cert | [$4,393](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30259333?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Confirm measurements for L/W too |
| 1ct G VS2 Ideal | Read cert | [$4,607](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29985422?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Cross-check depth and table % |

The rule: always open the full GIA report and read Table % and Depth %. Don't assume "Ideal" cut on the listing means ideal proportions. Retailers assign cut grades independently. The GIA report tells the real story.

> **"Depth percentage is the first thing I check on a marquise cert after carat weight. A beautiful G VS2 marquise can look dull and small if it's cut at 64% depth. I've seen clients compare two 1ct marquise stones at the same color and clarity — one at 60% depth and one at 64% depth — and immediately pick the shallower one. They don't know why. It's because the shallower stone is actually bigger face-up. The depth is doing all the work."**
> — Farzana Hasan, diamond expert | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

**Verdict:** For every marquise on your shortlist, open the GIA report PDF. Check Depth %. Under 62% is good. Over 63% is a face-up penalty. Table % between 53–63% is correct. The price won't tell you this — the cert will.

## GIA vs IGI for Marquise

The [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) report is the gold standard for natural marquise diamonds. The [IGI](https://www.igi.org/) report is the standard for [lab-grown marquise diamonds](/lab-grown-marquise-diamond). Understanding the difference matters for buyers shopping both markets.

**GIA for natural marquise:**
- The most conservative grading body; grades correlate to the most reliable color and clarity descriptions
- Dealers and appraisers price GIA-graded stones at a premium because the grade is trusted
- A GIA VS2 means VS2; there is no grade inflation on the GIA scale

**IGI for lab-grown marquise:**
- The most common cert for lab diamonds in the certified market
- IGI is slightly less conservative than GIA; a GIA VS2 and an IGI VS2 may not look identical
- For lab diamonds, IGI certification is the accepted standard across most retailers

**What this means for marquise buyers:**

If you're buying natural, choose GIA. If the listing says EGL or another minor lab, avoid it. Or require an independent appraisal. If you're buying lab, IGI is fine. GIA lab certs exist but are harder to find and come at a premium.

Browse certified natural GIA marquise: [GIA Marquise Diamond Search](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CertificateToggle=yes&Lab=GIA&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

Browse certified lab marquise (GIA, IGI, GCAL): [Lab Marquise Diamond Search](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds/lab-grown-diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CertificateToggle=yes&Lab=GIA,IGI,GCAL&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

## GIA Doesn't Grade Cut for Marquise

This is the most important thing most buyers don't know. GIA's Cut grade — Excellent, Very Good, Good, Fair, Poor — applies exclusively to round brilliant diamonds.

If you see an "Excellent Cut" GIA grade on a marquise listing, that is the retailer's own label. GIA did not assign it. The GIA report will show Polish and Symmetry grades. There is no Cut grade line for marquise.

Retailers compensate by applying their own cut assessment to fancy shapes. Retailers rate marquise cut quality as Ideal, Very Good, Good, or Fair using their own proportion analysis. This is useful but it is not a GIA grade.

**What to check instead of "Cut" on a marquise GIA cert:**
1. Polish: Excellent or Very Good is the target
2. Symmetry: Excellent or Very Good is the target
3. Proportion diagram: check Depth % and Table % yourself (covered above)

A marquise with GIA Polish: Excellent, Symmetry: Excellent, depth 58–62%, and table 53–63% is exceptionally cut. The absence of a GIA Cut grade does not mean quality is unknown. It means you have to read two more fields yourself.

> **"Every week I have a client ask me why their marquise GIA cert doesn't show an 'Excellent' cut grade. They read about Excellent cut being important and they can't find it on their cert. I explain that GIA only cuts grades round brilliants — for marquise, the Polish and Symmetry grades tell the story. A VG/VG polish and symmetry with good proportions is a beautiful stone. The missing 'Cut' line doesn't mean the stone is poorly cut."**
> — Farzana Hasan, diamond expert | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

**Verdict:** Don't filter by "Excellent GIA Cut" for marquise. It won't find what you're looking for. Filter by Polish: Excellent/Very Good and Symmetry: Excellent/Very Good. Then open the proportion diagram.

## What "Very Good" Cut Means on a Marquise Listing

When a retailer labels a marquise as "Very Good" cut, they're applying their own proportion analysis. Not a GIA grade.

A "Very Good" marquise cut grade typically means:
- Depth % within a reasonable range (usually 58–63%)
- Table % within a reasonable range (usually 53–63%)
- Polish and Symmetry both Very Good or better on the GIA cert

The "Ideal" cut grade tightens those ranges:
- Depth % 58–62%
- Table % 53–60%
- Polish and Symmetry both Excellent

In practice, the difference between a "Very Good" and "Ideal" marquise at the same grade is small. Both can be excellent stones. A Very Good at $4,049 can look identical to an Ideal at $4,607 in a [marquise diamond ring](/marquise-diamond-ring). Polish: Excellent, Symmetry: Very Good — the difference is small.

The cert proportion data tells you which stone is actually better cut. The label is a shortcut. The cert is the data.

