## Expert Summary

**By Farzana Hasan, diamond expert** | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

The GIA report for a [marquise diamond](/marquise-cut-diamond) holds more buying data than the listing grade letters. Most buyers stop at the letters. Four cert fields protect against overpaying by $2,000. Or buying a stone that looks smaller than it should.

Color field: G-H is the sweet spot. D-F charges a premium invisible in a marquise setting. Choose based on metal color — white gold wants G-H, yellow gold accepts H-I.

Clarity field: VS2 is the ceiling. VVS2 and above costs $1,000–$2,095 more at 1ct with zero visible improvement. The $2,095 gap between F VS2 and F VVS2 proves what reading this field saves.

Measurements field: Calculate L/W ratio. Divide Length by Width. 1.85–2.00:1 is classic marquise proportion. Outside that range, demand is thinner and the elongating effect is compromised.

Proportion diagram: Check Depth % and Table %. Depth over 62% hides carat weight below the girdle. Table over 63% reduces fire. Both fields appear on every GIA cert. Both take 15 seconds to check.

**Pros of GIA certified marquise:**
- Industry-standard grading; grades are reliable and consistent
- GIA report includes proportion data not available from listing photos
- [Resale value](/marquise-diamond-resale-value) is 20–30% higher with GIA cert than uncertified
- Lab or natural — GIA applies the same grading standards

**Cons:**
- GIA doesn't grade Cut for marquise — Polish and Symmetry replace it
- Reading the full cert requires 10 minutes and basic division
- VVS2 and D-E-F grades tempt buyers into overpaying; the cert shows why it's unnecessary

## Final Verdict

The GIA certificate for a marquise diamond is a buying tool, not a receipt. The grade letters are the shortcut. The proportion data, measurements, and clarity field are where the $2,000 savings live.

**Sweet spot at 1ct:** G VS2 at [$4,607](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29985422?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) or H VS2 at [$4,393](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30259333?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Verify depth under 62%, table 53–63%, L/W ratio 1.85–2.00:1. That stone in a [marquise diamond necklace](/marquise-diamond-necklace), [marquise diamond earrings](/marquise-diamond-earrings), or [marquise engagement ring](/marquise-diamond-engagement-ring) looks identical to the D VVS2 at $7,000+.

**Sweet spot at 0.5ct:** G VVS2 at [$873](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29320684?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) or I VVS2 at [$822](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29422863?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Check measurements for L/W ratio before buying.

| Buying Goal | Recommendation | Cert Check | What this means |
|-------------|---------------|------------|-----------------|
| Best value 1ct | G-H VS2 | Depth <62%, Table 53–63%, L/W 1.85–2.00 | Saves $835–$2,095 over D-F VVS2 |
| Maximize size | Depth 58–60%, any G-H VS2 | Shallowest cut in G-H tier | More face-up area per carat |
| White metal setting | G or H color | Color grade only | D-F adds nothing visible in platinum/white gold |
| Yellow gold setting | H or I color | Color grade only | Warm metal hides stone warmth — go one grade lower |
| Lab marquise | G-H VS2, IGI or GIA cert | Same four fields apply | Same savings logic; 40–70% lower price |

**Verdict:** Read the cert. Color field → G-H. Clarity field → VS2. Measurements field → L/W 1.85–2.00:1. Proportion diagram → Depth <62%, Table 53–63%. Four fields. Fifteen minutes. Up to $2,095 saved.

