## Frequently Asked Questions

### What does GIA certified mean for a marquise diamond?

A GIA certified marquise diamond has been graded by the Gemological Institute of America. It's the most respected grading lab in the industry. The GIA report documents carat weight, color, clarity, polish, symmetry, measurements, and proportion data. For marquise, GIA does not assign a Cut grade (that applies to round brilliant only). The report includes Polish and Symmetry grades instead.

### Does GIA give a cut grade for marquise diamonds?

No. GIA's Cut grade applies exclusively to round brilliant diamonds. For marquise and all fancy shapes, GIA reports Polish and Symmetry. No overall Cut grade is assigned. Retailers apply their own cut quality labels (Ideal, Very Good) based on proportion analysis. Always verify by reading the proportion data on the GIA cert itself.

### What color grade is best for a GIA marquise diamond?

G or H is the sweet spot for [marquise diamonds](/marquise-cut-diamond) in white metal (platinum or white gold). The elongated shape shows color more at the pointed tips. Go one grade higher here than you would in a round. D-F color provides no visible improvement in a set marquise. In yellow gold, H or I color is acceptable because the warm metal covers stone warmth.

### What clarity grade should I choose for a GIA marquise diamond?

VS2 is the recommended ceiling for marquise. VS2 inclusions are not visible to the naked eye in a marquise diamond. VVS2, VVS1, IF, and FL grades cost $1,000–$2,095 more at 1ct with zero visible difference. That $2,095 gap between F VS2 and F VVS2 proves exactly why VS2 is the smart choice.

### How do I calculate the L/W ratio from a GIA certificate?

Find the Measurements field on the GIA report. It shows three numbers in millimeters: Length × Width × Depth. Divide the first number (Length) by the second number (Width). A result between 1.85 and 2.00 is the ideal marquise proportion. For example, 9.21 × 4.63 mm gives 9.21 ÷ 4.63 = 1.99:1 — ideal. Outside this range, the stone is unusually narrow or wide.

### What table and depth percentages should I look for in a marquise diamond?

On the GIA proportion diagram, look for Table % between 53–63% and Depth % between 58–62%. Depth over 62% means carat weight hides below the girdle. The stone looks smaller face-up than it should. Table over 63% reduces fire and makes the stone look flat. Both fields appear on every GIA report. Check them before buying.

### Is GIA or IGI better for a marquise diamond?

GIA is the preferred certification for natural marquise diamonds. GIA is the most conservative grading lab. Certified stones resell for 20–30% more than uncertified ones. IGI is the standard for lab-grown marquise diamonds and is widely accepted. For natural stones, always prefer GIA. For lab stones, IGI certification is fine and GIA lab certs are also available.

### How much does a GIA certified 1ct marquise diamond cost?

From current certified inventory, GIA 1ct marquise diamonds range from [$4,049](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28386475?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) (I VS2) to $6,568+ (F VVS2). The G VS2 sweet spot is [$4,607](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29985422?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). H VS2 is [$4,393](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30259333?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Both offer the same visible quality as D VVS2 stones at $2,000+ more.

### Does fluorescence affect a GIA marquise diamond's appearance?

For D-F color marquise, avoid Strong or Very Strong fluorescence. It can look milky in UV-heavy lighting. For G-I color, faint or medium blue is fine. It often reduces price 10–20% with no downside. In some H-I color stones, medium blue fluorescence slightly improves face-up appearance by offsetting warmth.

### Why are some marquise diamonds cheaper than others at the same grade?

Three factors lower price outside the standard 4Cs. First: unfavorable L/W ratio (extreme narrow or wide proportions reduce buyer demand). Second: high depth % (deep-cut stones hide carat weight; buyers resist them). Third: strong fluorescence in colorless grades (D-F buyers avoid it). All three are visible on the GIA cert. A marquise priced $500 below market average often has one of these issues.

### Should I buy a GIA certified marquise in a bezel or prong setting?

Setting choice doesn't change which cert you need — but it changes which cert fields matter most. A [bezel setting](/bezel-setting-diamond) covers the girdle and tips, so girdle thickness is less visible. A prong setting exposes tips, making a clean VS2 more important (inclusions near tips are more visible). For either setting, G-H color and VS2 clarity remain the targets. Verify L/W ratio and depth % regardless of setting type.

### What is the length-to-width ratio on a GIA report for marquise?

GIA doesn't calculate L/W ratio directly — you calculate it yourself from the Measurements field. Divide Length ÷ Width from the mm dimensions. For a [marquise cut](/marquise-cut-diamond), 1.85:1 to 2.00:1 is the ideal range. Stones at 1.75:1 look wider and squatter. Stones at 2.10:1+ look very narrow. The classic elongating effect that makes marquise distinctive is strongest in the 1.85–2.00:1 range.

### Is a GIA certified marquise diamond worth more at resale?

Yes. A GIA certificate adds $300–$600 to resale value on a 1ct natural marquise. That's compared to an uncertified stone of the same quality. Dealers bid 20–30% higher on GIA-certified stones because they don't have to price in re-grading risk. If you ever plan to sell, the GIA certificate pays for itself at resale. See our [marquise diamond resale value guide](/marquise-diamond-resale-value) for the full breakdown.
